\section{Evaluation Variance Analysis}
\label{app:eval_variance}

To quantify evaluation noise at $\tau{=}0.4$, we evaluate a single checkpoint (seed200, step~5) three times independently on the 134-task OOD set with different random seeds.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Evaluation variance for a single checkpoint (seed200 step~5, 134 OOD tasks, $\tau{=}0.4$). Three independent runs yield std$=$1.29pp, an order of magnitude smaller than the inter-seed performance range in Table~\ref{tab:seed_variability} (0\%--30.6\%).}
\label{tab:eval_variance}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Checkpoint} & \textbf{Runs} & \textbf{OOD Mean (\%)} & \textbf{Std (pp)} & \textbf{Range (pp)} \\
\midrule
seed200 step~5 & 3 & 11.94 & 1.29 & 2.24 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The small standard deviation (1.29pp) confirms that the large inter-seed differences observed in Table~\ref{tab:seed_variability}---ranging from 0\% (collapsed) to 30.6\% (generalized)---reflect genuine training dynamics rather than evaluation noise.

\section{Use of AI Assistants}
\label{app:ai-assistants}

We used Claude (Anthropic) as an AI coding and writing assistant
throughout this work. Specifically:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Code:} drafting experiment scripts, data-processing
    pipelines, and analysis utilities; debugging and refactoring.
    \item \textbf{Writing:} drafting and revising paper sections,
    polishing prose, and reformatting LaTeX.
    \item \textbf{Literature:} summarizing related papers to inform
    related-work section.
\end{itemize}

